messages:
emma: Heads-up — the JS quickstart is tripping up new users. README shows `const Redwood = require('@redwood/inference')` but the published package exports a named `RedwoodClient`. Also the Edge example mixes `import` and `require` in the same file and I hit `ReferenceError: require is not defined` on Vercel Edge.

liam: Yep ran into this yesterday. Docs imply a default export while the package is ESM-friendly with named exports. If you `require()` you get an object like `{ RedwoodClient: [Function] }` so folks expect `const client = new Redwood()` but should be `const { RedwoodClient } = require(...)` in CJS.

samir: TS chips in — the quickstart uses `new Redwood()` in the code sample, but types declare `RedwoodClient`. In strict TS projects that breaks immediately.

maya: Short-term UX fix: show both CJS and ESM examples and clearly label which is Edge-safe (ESM only). Also show how to pass the API key in an edge-friendly way (no dynamic require on module init).

emma: Example copy I pulled from README: 
```js
// README snippet
const Redwood = require('@redwood/inference');
const client = new Redwood(process.env.RW_API_KEY);
```
error on Vercel Edge: `ReferenceError: require is not defined`

liam: Proposed doc edits: 1) add an "Edge runtime" quickstart that only uses ESM `import` and named exports, 2) keep a Server (CJS) quickstart separately, 3) clarify package.json fields (`main` vs `module`) so bundlers know which build to pick.

samir: Also a note on auth/retries — some serverless examples do `const client = new RedwoodClient({ apiKey: await fetchSecret() })` at module scope which can return undefined during cold start and causes 401s. Docs should recommend resolving secrets synchronously before instantiating client or lazy-initializing inside handler and show a tiny retry wrapper.

maya: Edge-safe auth snippet I drafted: 
```js
import { RedwoodClient } from '@redwood/inference';

const client = new RedwoodClient({
  apiKey: Deno?.env?.RW_API_KEY || process.env?.RW_API_KEY
});

export default async (req) => {
  try {
    const out = await client.generate({ prompt: 'hi' });
    return new Response(JSON.stringify(out));
  } catch (err) {
    // simple retry once for transient auth fetch races
  }
};
```

liam: One more nuance — bundlers like webpack/rollup sometimes prefer default imports. We should either add a default re-export in the package (e.g., `export default RedwoodClient`) or explicitly document the alias patterns so users don't keep guessing.

emma: Could adding a default export break tree-shaking/typing? If it's safe, might reduce a lot of support emails.

samir: It can be done without major cost. I'll open an SDK ticket proposing `export default RedwoodClient` and a tiny changelog note.

maya: I'll update quickstart PR to include: 
- ESM Edge example (recommended for Vercel/Cloudflare)
- CJS server example (Node servers)
- Troubleshooting entry: "Cannot use require in Edge" + common fixes
- TS note: use `RedwoodClient` type or alias in doc examples

dev-docs-bot: Draft PR: https://github.com/redwood-inference/docs/pull/842 (edge/imports) — staging preview: https://deploy.preview/docs/quickstart-import-style :eyes:

noah-bot: Logged RDWX-512 to product board: "DX docs: clarify import styles and Edge examples"

emma: Great, thanks team — removing friction here will cut a bunch of support tickets. :thumbsup: 

liam: Can someone add a tiny table in the quickstart summarizing import patterns and runtime compatibility? e.g.,
- Node (CJS): `const { RedwoodClient } = require('@redwood/inference')`
- Node (ESM): `import { RedwoodClient } from '@redwood/inference'`
- Edge: `import { RedwoodClient } from '@redwood/inference'` + no `require`

samir: I'll include that in the PR.

emma: Thanks — marking this as docs action. Will follow up if we need SDK change as well.
